Evan released it only after the lid pressed against his fingers.

They packed in silence. Abby held the recorder where the lowered camera could no longer document it. Ron carried the tripod. Diane pushed the cart toward the junction while Evan walked beside the laptop bag, guarding it with the care he would have given a witness.

At the staff stair, the fire door behind them eased open by an inch.

The closer held it there.

A low rush moved through the gap.

No one turned.

Diane counted each person through the stair door and closed it after herself.

Four entered the stairwell.

From the basement side came one final sound, thin enough to have crossed the return duct.

“—turned around—”

The fragment carried Evan’s cadence without preserving the sentence around it. The closed computer rested inside the bag against his hip.

Evan stopped on the first step.

Diane pushed him upward.

“Keep moving.”

# **Scene 6 — The West Route**

Diane led them through the west stairwell with Ron behind the students and Abby’s camera pointed at the floor. The red recording light remained visible in the reflection from the handrail. Evan carried the closed laptop under one arm. Its power cable hung from his fist, the plug striking each riser with a small plastic knock.

The basement door closed beneath them.

For the first landing, everyone listened to the door.

A pressure seal caught, released, and caught again. The latch entered its plate with a flat metallic click. Air from the upper corridor moved down the stairwell and lifted the edge of a fire-inspection notice. Ron stopped long enough to test the handle.

“Secure,” he said.

Diane continued upward.

The stairwell smelled of concrete dust and the lemon cleaner used on the public floors. The shift from boiler heat to conditioned air brought a thin chill across the sweat beneath her collar. Above, the school’s wider corridors and occupied rooms restored a sense of scale. A basketball struck hardwood in the fieldhouse. A public-address speaker released half a syllable before somebody cut the microphone. Pipes carried water toward the locker rooms.

Evening activities continued around them. Diane welcomed each ordinary sound and named none.

Evan broke the silence first.

“You heard it again.”

Ron’s hand closed around the rail.

Diane kept climbing. “I heard a fragment of your own voice.”

“From the basement after we closed the door.”

“The return grille carried your voice twice earlier. Hard walls and a closing stairwell can preserve a fragment.”

Evan shifted the laptop against his ribs. “You always have a category ready.”

“Categories keep people from turning fear into instructions.”

“That sound gave an instruction.”

“Your earlier question supplied one.”

Abby raised the camera toward Diane’s back. The lens made a soft internal adjustment.

Ron said, “Lower it.”

Abby lowered it at once.

They reached the first-floor door. Diane opened it onto the old west hall, a narrow corridor built before the larger academic wing. Trophy photographs occupied one wall. On the other, painted doors opened into storage rooms, a costume shop, and the passage leading behind the auditorium. The ceiling sat lower here, crossed by boxed ducts installed after the original construction.

The nearest exit lay through the lobby, left at the next junction.

Diane turned right.

Ron noticed. “Lobby is the other way.”

“I need the recorder logged in the auditorium office. The main office is closed, and the assistant principal left for the district meeting.”

“We can lock it in facilities.”

“The auditorium office has the media safe and a working copier. Abby’s card needs duplication before anyone handles the camera again.”

Evan looked along the corridor. “The auditorium.”

“Five minutes,” Diane said. “You remain together. The camera stays pointed down unless I request documentation.”

“You want evidence preserved now.”

“I want a record that survives your editing.”

The words carried farther than she intended. They passed into the boxed duct above the costume-room door and returned as a softer rhythm.

Editing.

Editing.

The second return barely resembled speech.

Abby glanced upward.

Diane pointed to the vent. “Metal enclosure. Hard walls. Short delay.”

Evan’s mouth formed a reply, then closed. For once, he allowed the explanation to remain.

They passed the costume shop. A row of garment racks stood behind wired glass, covered in clear plastic that reflected Abby’s camera light in vertical bands. Farther on, the corridor widened beside a set of double doors marked AUDITORIUM. A hand-painted sign advertised the spring musical. Beneath it, strips of masking tape marked where old posters had been removed.

Ron reached for the push bar.

A piano note sounded beyond the doors.

One low tone.

The note held for less than a second, followed by the faint wooden complaint of a pedal returning.

Ron’s hand stayed above the bar.

Evan whispered, “Camera up.”

Abby looked to Diane.

Diane listened.

The auditorium ventilation ran at evening setback. Air moved quietly through the ceiling diffusers. No rehearsal appeared on the building schedule. The music department used the stage piano, but the instrument usually rested in the backstage alcove beneath a quilted cover. A student could still occupy the room. A teacher could have entered through the house doors. The note could have traveled from a practice room through the shared return.

Diane took out her radio.

“Control, location check for auditorium and music wing.”

Static came through. Then the security aide’s voice: “Main building clear except fieldhouse practice. Music rooms locked at four-ten. Auditorium showed clear on last pass.”

“What time?”

“Four twenty-seven.”

Diane checked the wall clock above the drinking fountain.

4:41.

“Copy.”

Evan watched the double doors. “Fourteen minutes is a long time.”

“It is also enough time for someone with a key.”

“Or something following a route.”

Diane turned toward him. “You have one note.”

A second note sounded.

Higher this time. Then a third, separated by the interval Wayne used to test whether a piano still held pitch after stage crews moved it. He knew no scales. He struck the same three keys before every concert setup: low, middle, high, listening for a buzz in the soundboard.

Diane felt the sequence before memory supplied its name.

Ron looked at her.

Evan caught both reactions. “What is it?”

“An instrument,” Diane said.

“What pattern?”

“Three notes.”

“You recognized them.”

“Anyone can recognize three notes.”

Ron pushed the bar. “We check the room, then everyone leaves.”

Inside, the auditorium extended into receding layers of unlit space. Emergency strips drew two weak lines toward the stage and left the seats between them without faces. The curtain hung half open, exposing a wedge of black floor and the pale edge of a scenery flat. Above, the lighting booth presented a dark rectangular opening at the rear of the house. Its old window had been removed years earlier after heat cracked the glass. The empty frame appeared blacker than the wall around it.

Diane switched on the house work lights.

Rows of seats emerged in muted red. Dust glimmered above the stage. The room looked vacant.

Abby filmed the aisles, the balcony rail, the stage, and the house doors in one slow sweep.

“State the time,” Diane said.

“Four forty-two and sixteen seconds.”

“Continuous from the hall?”

“Yes.”

“Show the door behind us.”

Abby turned. Ron stood beside the closed double doors. Evan remained within arm’s reach of him, laptop still tucked under one arm.

Diane entered the center aisle.

“Hello?” she called.

Her voice reached the stage, rose into the fly space, and returned from the rear wall.

No sound followed.

She walked toward the proscenium. Each step sank into the aisle carpet and produced almost no sound. Behind her, Evan’s shoes squeaked in a repeating pair. Abby’s were softer. Ron’s keys remained still in his pocket.

At row H, something moved backstage.

A short wooden scrape crossed from left to right.

Evan stopped. “Chair.”

“Could be a stand,” Abby said.

“Could be the piano bench,” Ron added.

Diane raised one hand. The group stopped with her.

Nothing moved within the stage opening.

A fourth piano note sounded, muffled and close.

The tone came from stage left.

Diane moved to the stairs.

Ron caught her sleeve. “We can call security.”

“Security will call me.”

“Then call another custodian.”

“I am the custodian.”

Diane heard Wayne’s old impatience in her own answer. He had said the same words whenever someone suggested waiting for maintenance while he already stood in the room with a wrench.

Diane removed Ron’s hand and climbed.

The stage floor held the cold, dry smell of dust, rope, old paint, and warm electrical cable. Tape marks crossed the boards in faded colors. Along the back wall, scenery flats created narrow passages invisible from the house. The quilted piano cover lay folded on a chair beside the stage-left entrance.

The piano was absent from its usual alcove.

Evan saw the empty space. “Where is it?”

“Could be in the rehearsal room,” Diane said.

Ron looked at the folded cover. “They moved it for the choir concert.”

“That was last week.”

“Could still be out.”

Diane crossed to the backstage corridor. A pair of fire doors divided the stage wing from the music passage. The nearer door stood open by three inches, held against its closer by pressure from the return system.

She pointed to the gap. “Door movement. Air pressure.”

The door widened another inch, then eased back.

Abby recorded the closer arm, the floor clearance, and a strip of paper trembling near the jamb.

Evan said, “That one has an explanation.”

“All doors have conditions.”

“Conditions are not explanations.”

“They are where explanations begin.”

A piano key struck beyond the fire doors.

The note carried little reverberation now. It came from a nearby enclosed space.

Diane pushed through.

The music passage curved behind the auditorium. On one side, locked practice rooms showed narrow vertical windows. On the other, storage cages held percussion equipment, folding risers, and music stands. At the far end, the passage joined the stage-loading corridor and the stairs to the lighting booth.

The upright piano stood halfway down the hall.

Its front faced the wall. One caster rested inside a shallow crack in the terrazzo, tilting the keyboard slightly toward the corridor. The bench sat three feet away. No person occupied the space between.

Abby framed the instrument and surrounding floor.

Evan whispered, “It moved.”

Ron answered, “Stage crew left it here.”

“When?”

“I don’t know.”

Diane walked around the piano. The fallboard was open. White keys reflected the overhead fluorescents. Dust lay across the top except where someone had rested a hand near the music rack.

She held her palm above the keys.

No air moved across them.

She inspected the practice-room doors. All four showed locked latches. The percussion cage remained secured with a district padlock. The loading corridor door stood closed at the far junction. A red exit indicator glowed above it.

“Camera on my hands,” Diane said.

Abby moved closer.

Diane pressed the three keys Wayne used.

Low.

Middle.

High.

The tones matched the sequence from the auditorium.

Evan looked toward the camera. “Same notes.”

“The same piano,” Diane said. “Three common keys.”

“Why those three?”

“To listen for vibration after moving it.”

“Who taught you that?”

Diane released the high key.

The fading tone left the corridor quiet.

Ron said, “Evan, leave it.”

Evan stepped beside the piano, careful to remain outside Diane’s reach. “Wayne checked the piano.”

Evan asked nothing.

Diane closed the fallboard.

“Wayne helped move stage equipment.”

“From the basement.”

“From wherever equipment required moving.”

“Basement storage. Auditorium stage. Piano corridor.”

Ron moved toward Evan. “That is enough.”

Evan raised one hand while keeping the other around the laptop. “And the lighting booth.”

The booth stairs stood at the far end of the passage.

Diane saw the route as Wayne had used it. Boiler room at shutdown. Storage bay for folding chairs and replacement bulbs. West stair to the stage. Piano check. Lighting booth for burned lamps and loose cables. Then back through the house to lock the lobby.

